def english_coding() -> list[dict]:
    return [
        rec("What is a REST API?", "A **REST API** (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style for building web services.\n\n**Core principles:**\n- **Stateless**: Each request contains all info needed\n- **Resource-based**: URLs represent resources (`/users/123`)\n- **HTTP methods**: GET (read), POST (create), PUT (update), DELETE (remove)\n- **JSON format**: Standard data exchange\n\n```python\n# FastAPI example\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get(\"/users/{user_id}\")\nasync def get_user(user_id: int):\n    return {\"id\": user_id, \"name\": \"Bi\"}\n\n@app.post(\"/users\")\nasync def create_user(name: str):\n    return {\"id\": 1, \"name\": name}\n```\n\n**Best practices:** Use plural nouns (`/users` not `/user`), proper status codes, versioning (`/api/v1/`).", "english_coding"),
        rec("Explain Docker in simple terms", "**Docker** = Lightweight virtual machine for your app.\n\nThink of it as a **shipping container** — your app + all dependencies packaged together. Works the same everywhere.\n\n```\nWithout Docker:     With Docker:\n\"Works on my       \"Works everywhere\" ✅\n machine\" 😅        \n```\n\n**Key concepts:**\n- **Image**: Blueprint (like a class)\n- **Container**: Running instance (like an object)\n- **Dockerfile**: Recipe to build an image\n- **Docker Compose**: Run multiple containers together\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.11-slim\nCOPY . /app\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]\n```\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t myapp .\ndocker run -p 8080:8080 myapp\n```", "english_coding"),
        rec("What are design patterns?", "**Design Patterns** = Proven solutions to common software problems.\n\n**3 categories:**\n\n**Creational** (object creation):\n- **Singleton**: Only one instance (database connection)\n- **Factory**: Create objects without specifying exact class\n- **Builder**: Step-by-step construction\n\n**Structural** (composition):\n- **Adapter**: Make incompatible interfaces work together\n- **Decorator**: Add behavior dynamically\n- **Facade**: Simple interface to complex system\n\n**Behavioral** (communication):\n- **Observer**: Notify subscribers of changes (event system)\n- **Strategy**: Swap algorithms at runtime\n- **Command**: Encapsulate requests as objects\n\n**Most used in Python:** Singleton, Factory, Observer, Strategy, Decorator (built into language!).", "english_coding"),
        rec("Git merge vs rebase?", "**Merge** — Creates a merge commit:\n```\nmain:    A---B---C---M\n              \\     /\nfeature:       D---E\n```\n\n**Rebase** — Replays commits on top:\n```\nmain:    A---B---C\n                  \\\nfeature:           D'---E'\n```\n\n**When to use what:**\n\n| | Merge | Rebase |\n|---|---|---|\n| History | Preserves all commits | Linear, clean |\n| Safety | Always safe | ⚠️ Don't rebase shared branches |\n| Use case | PR merges, team work | Local cleanup before PR |\n\n```bash\n# Merge\ngit checkout main && git merge feature\n\n# Rebase (on your local branch)\ngit checkout feature && git rebase main\n```\n\n**Golden rule**: Never rebase commits that others have pulled.", "english_coding"),
    ]


